  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...There is little change in the synoptic setup as\nan upper level trough currently in the Tennessee River Valley \nhas stalled across the area and will remain there into midweek \nbefore weakening and eventually getting absorbed by a passing \nnorthern stream trough Wed/Thurs. This should keep the moisture \nchannel open from the Gulf into at least midweek as upper level \nS-SW'rly winds remain in place advecting moisture N'wards. This \nsecond trough should remain well enough to the north to preclude\nany impacts to the area later this week. At the same time we \nstill expect a few shortwaves to transit around the base of this\ntrough and lift north through the Carolinas today and potentially\nagain on Wed. As the upper trough dissipates/gets absorbed \nlater this week any mid level forcing does look to shift further\ninland away from ENC. \n\nWhat does this mean for us as far as ambient weather goes through \nWed? Stalled surface front to our west will move little before \ndissipating on Wed. A shortwave transits across the area \nthis morning bringing a renewed threat for more widespread \nshower and thunderstorm activity, initially along the coast but\nslowly progressing inland. While the flooding risk is localized\nto areas where heavy rain rates stall, that does not mean it is\nzero as any shower or storm will have a chance to produce \nrainfall rates of at least 1-2\" an hour which could yield an \nisolated and localized threat for flooding in slower moving \nstorms. In fact, a Flash Flood Warning is in effect for Northern\nHatteras Island as 3-6\" inches of rain have fallen in the past\n2-3 hours in the area. Any addition areas where localized flash\nflooding could occur are confined to along the Crystal Coast \nand along the OBX south of Oregon Inlet.\n\nWe are now in a Marginal (level 1/4) Excessive Rain Outlook for\ntoday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...By the end of the week it looks like we return \nback to climo or below climo PoPs as a lack of mid level \nshortwaves or significant forcing will be noted across ENC. This\nwill likely result in chances for showers and thunderstorms \nalong the daily seabreeze but once again these chances should be\nisolated at best given the overall setup. After a cool start to\nthe week given widespread cloudcover and rain as highs only get\ninto the low to mid 80s and lows in the low 70s through Wed, we\ndo begin to warm back up with temps returning to the upper 80s \nto low 90s inland and mid 80s along the OBX.",
